    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>By the 1780s Saint-Domingue produced about 40 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and 60 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> consumed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, more than all the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> together, and its taxes were a major source of the French budget.</li><li>Between 1681 and 1791 an estimated 790,000 to 860,000 enslaved Africans were brought to its plantations, a third of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> in 1783–1791; some 5 to 10 per cent died every year, so the population was kept up only by constant importation.</li><li>Enslaved Africans and Creoles planned their rising at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> ceremony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bois-caiman">Bois Caïman</a> in 1791; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1793, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>'s constitution of 1801 declared it abolished for ever, and the French army sent to restore it was beaten at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vertieres">Battle of Vertières</a> on 18 November 1803.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1802, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a> warned his captors: &quot;In overthrowing me you have cut down in Saint-Domingue only the trunk of the tree of liberty; it will spring up again from the roots, for they are numerous and they are deep.&quot;</p></aside>
    </section>

